			<div class="content" id="post-content-1490830">
									

<p>I'll start with my one real issue with this article:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The original copy of SCP-1314-B is to be stored in a clearly marked A4 Manilla envelope kept in Dr. ██████’s office.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I know that a lot of the older articles have this kind of containment, but it feels weird when considered against current standards. I would think that the Foundation would want tighter control over a potentially lethal SCP that can easily be abused. I would suggest putting it in a locked container with a security log or something.</p>
<p>Now, that being said, I really like the concept. My first read-through, I was afraid it was going to be something that makes you smarter for no reason, but the progressive side effects of simulating this model on it victims was pretty cool. Your tone is also good and the article is well-written. I hesitate to call attention to a writer's experience, since I fervently believe that all articles on the website should be judged on their merits alone, but this is a good article in general, and an excellent first article.</p>
<p>Well done. +1</p>

